---
import ComparisonPageLayout from '../../layouts/ComparisonPageLayout.astro';

// The Warp claims changed in this refresh were checked against Warp's docs,
// repo and blog on 2026-10-11; sources are in the verification block below.
// Routing: state only the documented personal custom-endpoint flow and
// disclose that Warp's pricing page describes BYOK differently. Never write
// "every Warp AI request". See
// docs/research/2026-10-11-competitive-landscape-refresh.md section 1.
const comparison = {
  competitor: {
    name: 'Warp',
    icon: '🚀',
    tagline: 'Agentic development environment (open-source client)',
  },
  tldr: {
    title: 'TL;DR',
    points: [
      'Warp open-sourced its terminal client in April 2026 under AGPL-3.0, the same license as Caro. Warp\'s FAQ says its server, Warp Drive backend, orchestration layer and agent harness are not in the repository.',
      'The Warp app works without an account, but Warp ties its AI features to a Warp account, and they need a network connection. The Agent CLI signs in separately. Warp\'s docs say custom model endpoints must be publicly reachable, so a local model needs a public tunnel. With its default embedded backend and a cached model, Caro needs no network and no account.',
      'The Warp app replaces your terminal. The Warp Agent CLI (August 2026) runs in any terminal, but needs a Warp account. Caro needs no account.',
      'Warp asks before agent commands that match an editable regex denylist. Caro checks every command against 52+ curated patterns, rates the risk, and at the default safety level blocks Critical commands.',
      'Warp is a full agentic workspace: agents, Drive, teams and cloud agents. Caro does one job: natural language to a safe shell command.',
    ]
  },
  headToHead: [
    { feature: 'Open source', caro: true, competitor: 'partial', winner: 'caro' },
    { feature: 'AI works fully offline', caro: true, competitor: false, winner: 'caro' },
    { feature: 'Local models without a tunnel', caro: true, competitor: false, winner: 'caro' },
    { feature: 'No account needed for AI', caro: true, competitor: false, winner: 'caro' },
    { feature: 'Curated dangerous-command patterns', caro: true, competitor: 'partial', winner: 'caro' },
    { feature: 'Risk level for each command', caro: true, competitor: 'unknown', winner: 'none' },
    { feature: 'Custom command rules (patterns, allow/deny lists)', caro: true, competitor: true, winner: 'tie' },
    { feature: 'AI usable from your existing terminal', caro: true, competitor: true, winner: 'tie' },
    { feature: 'macOS, Linux and Windows', caro: true, competitor: true, winner: 'tie' },
    { feature: 'Full coding agent (edits files, runs tasks)', caro: false, competitor: true, winner: 'competitor' },
    { feature: 'Team collaboration (Drive, session sharing)', caro: false, competitor: true, winner: 'competitor' },
    { feature: 'Modern terminal UI (blocks, tabs)', caro: false, competitor: true, winner: 'competitor' },
  ],
  sections: [
    {
      title: 'What Is Open Source',
      icon: '❯',
      content: `
        <p><strong>Warp</strong> published its client on GitHub on April 28, 2026. Most of the code is AGPL-3.0, and the UI framework crates are MIT. Warp's FAQ says the server, the Warp Drive backend, the Oz orchestration layer and the built-in agent harness are not in the repository.</p>
        <p><strong>Caro</strong> is AGPL-3.0 from end to end: the CLI, the safety patterns, the prompts and the inference code. Its default model, Qwen2.5-Coder 1.5B, is open-weight under Apache-2.0. You can read, audit and change every part that decides which command you see.</p>
      `
    },
    {
      title: 'Your Terminal',
      icon: '>_',
      content: `
        <p><strong>The Warp app</strong> is its own terminal emulator, so you move from iTerm2, Ghostty, Terminal.app or Windows Terminal to Warp. <strong>The Warp Agent CLI</strong>, launched in August 2026, runs inside any terminal and over SSH. It needs a Warp login or API key.</p>
        <p><strong>Caro</strong> is a single CLI with optional shell integration for zsh, bash and fish. It does not replace your terminal, your prompt or your config, and it needs no account.</p>
      `
    },
    {
      title: 'Privacy and Connectivity',
      icon: '▸',
      content: `
        <p><strong>Warp</strong> needs a network connection for its AI features; without one, the terminal works but the AI does not. The app works without an account, but Warp ties AI features to a Warp account. For a personal custom model endpoint, Warp's docs (updated October 2026) say the prompt, context, endpoint URL and key pass through Warp, and that localhost and private network URLs are rejected. Warp's pricing page describes bring-your-own-key differently, and Enterprise BYOLLM has its own terms, so check the current terms for your plan. On the Free plan, telemetry is on by default and must stay on to use AI. On Business and Enterprise it is off by default. Warp has zero-data-retention agreements with its model providers.</p>
        <p><strong>Caro</strong>, with its default embedded backend, runs the model on your machine. After a one-time model download, prompts and commands do not leave it, so Caro works on a plane, behind a strict VPN or in an air-gapped network. Optional remote backends (Ollama, vLLM and others) send prompts to the server you choose.</p>
      `
    },
    {
      title: 'Safety Approach',
      icon: '■',
      content: `
        <p><strong>Warp</strong> gives each agent action an autonomy level: Agent decides, Always ask, Always allow or Never. In normal autonomy modes, commands that match a regex denylist need your approval. The default list covers tools like <code>rm</code>, <code>curl</code>, <code>eval</code> and <code>ssh</code>. In "Run until completion" mode, your own denylist is bypassed by default; admin denylist rules are not. A match asks; it does not block.</p>
        <p><strong>Caro</strong> checks every command against 52+ curated patterns and rates it Safe, Moderate, High or Critical. At the default safety level it blocks Critical commands, such as <code>rm -rf /</code> and fork bombs, whatever the model suggests, and asks before High ones. You can add your own patterns and allowlist entries in the <code>[safety]</code> config.</p>
      `
    },
    {
      title: 'Use Both',
      icon: '▸',
      content: `
        <p>Caro is a plain CLI, so it runs inside Warp like in any other terminal. Use Warp for its agents and workspace. Use Caro when you want a quick, offline, safety-checked command.</p>
      `
    },
  ],
  verdict: {
    chooseCaro: [
      'You want to keep your terminal and your setup',
      'You need AI that works offline or air-gapped',
      'You do not want an account or telemetry to use AI',
      'You want a curated safety library with risk levels, not only a denylist',
      'You want the whole stack open, inference code and default model included',
    ],
    chooseCompetitor: [
      'You want a full agentic development environment',
      'You want hosted frontier models and cloud agents',
      'Team features like Drive and session sharing matter to you',
      'You like a modern terminal UI with blocks and tabs',
    ]
  },
  verification: {
    date: '2026-10-11',
    sources: [
      { label: 'Warp blog: Warp is now open-source (Apr 28, 2026)', url: 'https://www.warp.dev/blog/warp-is-now-open-source' },
      { label: 'warpdotdev/warp repository and FAQ', url: 'https://github.com/warpdotdev/warp' },
      { label: 'Warp docs: custom inference endpoints', url: 'https://docs.warp.dev/agents/inference/custom-inference-endpoint/' },
      { label: 'Warp docs: bring your own API key', url: 'https://docs.warp.dev/agents/inference/bring-your-own-api-key/' },
      { label: 'Warp pricing (BYOK wording differs from the docs)', url: 'https://www.warp.dev/pricing' },
      { label: 'Warp docs: using Warp offline', url: 'https://docs.warp.dev/support-and-community/troubleshooting-and-support/using-warp-offline/' },
      { label: 'Warp docs: privacy', url: 'https://docs.warp.dev/support-and-community/privacy-and-security/privacy/' },
      { label: 'Warp docs: agent profiles and permissions', url: 'https://docs.warp.dev/agents/capabilities/agent-profiles-permissions/' },
      { label: 'Warp blog: Introducing the Warp Agent CLI (Aug 4, 2026)', url: 'https://www.warp.dev/blog/introducing-the-warp-agent-cli-coding-agent' },
      { label: 'Warp docs: installation and setup', url: 'https://docs.warp.dev/getting-started/quickstart/installation-and-setup/' },
      { label: 'Warp changelog 2026 (AI features tied to a Warp account, Jul 3)', url: 'https://docs.warp.dev/changelog/2026/' },
    ]
  }
};
---

<ComparisonPageLayout
  title="Caro vs Warp - Feature Comparison | Terminal AI Tools"
  description="Compare Caro and Warp: an open-source, local-by-default shell CLI with built-in safety checks vs an agentic development environment with an open-source client and hosted AI."
  comparison={comparison}
  heroSubtitle="Keep your terminal. Keep your data on your machine."
  finalCtaHeading="Keep Your Terminal. Add AI Safely."
  finalCtaSubtext="Caro runs in the terminal you already use. No account, no cloud required."
/>
